WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:08.424
This is Chapter Six, Episode Twelve: customs and days of observance.

3
00:00:08.974 --> 00:00:16.386
In Denmark, a number of public holidays, days of observance and special customs are celebrated through the year.

4
00:00:16.337 --> 00:00:21.574
On many of these days children have the day off from school and adults have the day off from work.

5
00:00:21.574 --> 00:00:28.482
Denmark is, by the way, one of the only countries in the world that does not have an actual official national day.

6
00:00:29.032 --> 00:00:30.369
Fastelavn.

7
00:00:30.319 --> 00:00:32.969
Fastelavn is another word for carnival.

8
00:00:32.969 --> 00:00:38.182
It is a spring festival that can be traced back to the ancient Roman Empire and Egypt.

9
00:00:38.182 --> 00:00:44.107
Fastelavn in Denmark was originally the entrance to the fasting period within the Christian faith.

10
00:00:44.107 --> 00:00:48.532
According to old custom, children dress up and hit a barrel.

11
00:00:48.532 --> 00:00:55.395
In the old days there was a live cat in the barrel, and the one who breaks the barrel is still called the cat queen or cat king.

12
00:00:55.394 --> 00:00:58.120
Today there is sweets and fruit in the barrel.

13
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.020
Fastelavn is seven weeks before Easter.

14
00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.157
Easter, påske.

15
00:01:03.108 --> 00:01:08.545
The Christian Easter marks that Jesus was crucified, died and rose from the dead.

16
00:01:08.545 --> 00:01:12.045
In Denmark Easter is often used for family parties.

17
00:01:12.045 --> 00:01:15.882
From old times there is a tradition of giving each other Easter eggs.

18
00:01:15.882 --> 00:01:18.282
The egg is an old fertility symbol.

19
00:01:18.282 --> 00:01:21.145
Today Easter eggs are often made of chocolate.

20
00:01:21.145 --> 00:01:30.795
In addition there is a tradition of eating lamb, since the lamb symbolises Easter's message that Jesus was sacrificed as an innocent lamb for people's sins.

21
00:01:30.795 --> 00:01:34.110
Easter falls every year in March or April.

22
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.073
The first of May.

23
00:01:36.023 --> 00:01:43.685
The labour movement's international day of struggle is celebrated with parades, political speeches and events all over the country.

24
00:01:43.685 --> 00:01:53.808
Both the trade union movement and the parties that stem from the trade union movement, the Social Democrats, SF and the Red-Green Alliance, are behind these events.

25
00:01:54.358 --> 00:01:56.133
Pentecost, pinse.

26
00:01:56.083 --> 00:02:03.458
With the Christian Pentecost, people celebrate that the Holy Spirit, according to the Bible, has come to earth.

27
00:02:03.458 --> 00:02:08.658
Pentecost was originally the end of seven weeks of joyful celebration after Easter.

28
00:02:08.658 --> 00:02:18.671
At Pentecost some have the tradition of getting up early to see the sun "dance" and of having a Pentecost lunch, for example by bringing a picnic basket to the forest.

29
00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:23.322
Pentecost falls every year in May or June, seven weeks after Easter.

30
00:02:23.872 --> 00:02:26.484
Constitution Day, grundlovsdag.

31
00:02:26.434 --> 00:02:35.934
The fifth of June is Constitution Day, when people meet and in speeches celebrate Denmark's democratic constitution, Danmarks Riges Grundlov.

32
00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:39.780
Denmark got its first constitution in 1849.

33
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.380
Sankthans, Saint John's.

34
00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:46.718
On the evening of the 23rd of June, Sankthans is celebrated.

35
00:02:46.718 --> 00:02:52.905
The background for celebrating Sankthans eve is a mix of pagan folk tradition and Christianity.

36
00:02:52.905 --> 00:02:57.905
The festival marks midsummer, and according to old custom bonfires are burned.

37
00:02:57.905 --> 00:03:07.494
The name Hans is an abbreviation of Johannes, and Sankthans is in a church context a marking of John the Baptist's birthday on the 24th of June.

38
00:03:08.044 --> 00:03:09.669
Christmas, julen.

39
00:03:09.619 --> 00:03:12.931
At Christmas, Jesus's birth is celebrated.

40
00:03:12.931 --> 00:03:20.219
Christmas Eve is the 24th of December, when families gather, eat together and give each other presents.

41
00:03:20.219 --> 00:03:24.306
Most dance around the Christmas tree and sing Christmas hymns.

42
00:03:24.306 --> 00:03:27.094
Many also go to church for the Christmas service.

43
00:03:27.094 --> 00:03:38.206
Christmas thus has roots both in Christianity and in pagan folk tradition with a marking of the winter solstice, when the day length is shortest, and from there we move towards lighter times.

44
00:03:38.206 --> 00:03:43.832
The 25th and 26th of December are called first and second Christmas Day.

45
00:03:43.832 --> 00:03:46.824
Here many families gather for Christmas lunch.

46
00:03:47.374 --> 00:03:49.086
New Year, nytår.

47
00:03:49.036 --> 00:03:53.536
New Year's Eve, the 31st of December, is the year's last day.

48
00:03:53.536 --> 00:03:57.961
Parties are typically held and fireworks are set off all over the country.

49
00:03:57.961 --> 00:04:04.554
On New Year's Day, the first of January, the vast majority of workplaces and shops are closed.

50
00:04:05.104 --> 00:04:07.941
Other holy days and days of observance.

51
00:04:07.891 --> 00:04:14.466
The increased cultural exchange in the world has also brought a number of other customs and days of observance to Denmark.

52
00:04:14.466 --> 00:04:18.229
Some of them are due to more Danes with foreign backgrounds having come.

53
00:04:18.229 --> 00:04:21.991
The Muslim fasting month Ramadan is marked in Denmark.

54
00:04:21.991 --> 00:04:32.004
So is the Feast of Sacrifice, Eid al-Adha, which marks the end of the annual pilgrimage to Mecca, and is one of the most important Islamic holidays.

55
00:04:32.554 --> 00:04:49.902
The most important holy day in the Jewish year is Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement, which is used for a personal spiritual cleansing, where you get the opportunity to put away any old enmity and ask for forgiveness as well as repent your sins and do penance through prayer and fasting.

56
00:04:50.452 --> 00:04:55.264
Likewise some celebrate Halloween on the 31st of October as in the USA.

57
00:04:55.214 --> 00:05:01.552
Children dress up as witches, ghosts or similar, and lanterns are made of hollowed-out pumpkins.

58
00:05:01.552 --> 00:05:10.477
In several places in the country carnival is also celebrated after a South American model, with dressing up, samba music and parades.

59
00:05:10.477 --> 00:05:14.136
The biggest carnival is held at Pentecost in Copenhagen.

60
00:05:14.686 --> 00:05:20.373
These days are called mærkedage, days of observance, and helligdage, public holidays.

61
00:05:20.323 --> 00:05:26.898
At fastelavn, children dress up and slå katten af tønden, hit the cat out of the barrel.

62
00:05:27.348 --> 00:05:29.844
Here is your recap for Episode Twelve.

63
00:05:29.844 --> 00:05:34.620
Denmark is one of the only countries without an actual official national day.

64
00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:44.244
Fastelavn is carnival, seven weeks before Easter, where children dress up and hit a barrel; the one who breaks it is the cat queen or cat king.

65
00:05:44.244 --> 00:05:53.148
Easter, in March or April, marks that Jesus was crucified and rose from the dead; people give Easter eggs and eat lamb.

66
00:05:53.148 --> 00:05:57.276
The first of May is the labour movement's international day of struggle.

67
00:05:57.276 --> 00:06:03.804
Pentecost, in May or June, is seven weeks after Easter, and celebrates the Holy Spirit.

68
00:06:03.804 --> 00:06:06.348
The fifth of June is Constitution Day.

69
00:06:06.348 --> 00:06:14.028
Sankthans on the 23rd of June is midsummer, with bonfires, and marks John the Baptist's birthday on the 24th.

70
00:06:14.028 --> 00:06:21.636
Christmas Eve is the 24th of December, and the 25th and 26th are first and second Christmas Day.

71
00:06:21.636 --> 00:06:24.876
New Year's Eve is the 31st of December.

72
00:06:24.876 --> 00:06:34.260
Other days include Ramadan, Eid al-Adha, Yom Kippur, Halloween on the 31st of October, and the carnival in Copenhagen at Pentecost.

73
00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.410
Now it is time for your exam practice.

74
00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.272
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

75
00:06:42.272 --> 00:06:49.758
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

76
00:06:50.308 --> 00:06:51.558
Question 1.

77
00:06:51.508 --> 00:06:54.845
How is Sankthans traditionally celebrated in Denmark?

78
00:06:54.845 --> 00:06:59.008
Option A: People gather in the evening and light a bonfire.

79
00:06:59.008 --> 00:07:02.983
Option B: People hold a party and give each other presents.

80
00:07:02.983 --> 00:07:07.104
Option C: People dress up and hit the cat out of the barrel.
[DA] Hvordan fejrer man traditionelt sankthans i Danmark?  A: Man samles om aftenen og tænder bål  |  B: Man holder fest og giver hinanden gaver  |  C: Man klæder sig ud og slår katten af tønden

81
00:07:13.654 --> 00:07:18.129
The answer is A: People gather in the evening and light a bonfire.

82
00:07:18.079 --> 00:07:22.506
It appeared in the June 2021 and November 2023 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Man samles om aftenen og tænder bål

83
00:07:23.056 --> 00:07:24.818
That's the end of this part.

84
00:07:24.768 --> 00:07:25.836
Well done.

